---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-attentive';
const title = `Yotpo Email & SMS to Attentive: Shopify recovery migration | ${site.name}`;
const desc = 'Evaluate Attentive after Yotpo Email & SMS: Shopify event coverage, subscriber permissions, cart links and ownership across email and SMS.';
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Is Yotpo Email & SMS still a live alternative to Attentive?', answer: 'Treat this as a migration evaluation. Yotpo scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025; its current service notice confirms that the SMS dashboard and background services are decommissioned. Reviews and Loyalty are separate products.' },
  { question: 'Can Attentive replace both recovery channels?', answer: 'Its documentation describes triggered cart emails and Shopify checkout emails, alongside text journeys. Confirm access to triggered email in your intended account before retiring a separate email sender.' },
  { question: 'Does syncing an email address sync permission?', answer: 'No. Attentive distinguishes forwarding email addresses from forwarding email marketing consent in its Shopify settings. Map permissions and suppressions separately for each channel and test an opted-out record.' },
  { question: 'Can I export my old Yotpo subscribers now?', answer: 'Do not assume dashboard access or a fresh export is available. Work from retained files and permission evidence, reconcile Shopify records and ask support about unresolved gaps without promising recovery.' },
  { question: 'Will a cart link restore the original cart exactly?', answer: 'Attentive documents dynamic Shopify cart permalinks and a static fallback. Later cart additions can appear. Exercise variants, quantities, discounts and fallback behavior before relying on a message preview.' },
  { question: 'Should SMS always follow an unopened email?', answer: 'An unopened email does not establish SMS permission or a need for another reminder. Route by eligible channel and buying state, then check purchase exits and all other active senders.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Moving Shopify recovery from Yotpo Email &amp; SMS to Attentive</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">The important decision is who owns the next reminder when the same shopper qualifies for email, SMS and more than one abandonment journey.</p>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the current migration context</h2>
        <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025 and named Attentive among its recommended partners. Its <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">current notice</a> says the SMS dashboard and background services are decommissioned. A partner recommendation is a reason to evaluate a destination, not a measured recovery advantage. Retaining Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty does not restore Email &amp; SMS.</p>
        <p>For a merchant replacing a combined program, Attentive is a candidate when the team wants to manage recovery across both channels. The alternative is to retain a separate email platform and assign SMS a narrower role. The combined approach can simplify ownership; the split approach preserves an established email operation but needs an explicit handoff. Neither architecture should be selected from an invented ranking or a generic claim about store size.</p>
        <h2>What the Shopify connection actually establishes</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041854231-Shopify">Shopify integration guide</a> describes a tag that captures product views, cart additions and purchases, plus settings for collecting abandoned checkouts and other Shopify data. It separately distinguishes forwarding email addresses from forwarding email marketing consent. Record those settings during setup; the presence of an address is insufficient evidence that the corresponding permission state was transferred.</p>
        <p>Attentive's <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408727834772-Emails-in-journeys">triggered-email documentation</a> lists cart abandonment and Shopify-only checkout abandonment, with access arranged through its team. Secure that access and the intended sending domain before planning to replace email. An SMS account alone should not be treated as proof that your email migration is ready.</p>
        <h2>Choose a channel ownership model</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Design</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Acceptance requirement</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Attentive owns recovery email and SMS</td><td>The team wants a common recovery journey and can move its email operation</td><td>Both channels are enabled, templates are ready and competing reminders are disabled</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing ESP owns email; Attentive owns SMS</td><td>Email deliverability, templates and broader lifecycle flows are already established</td><td>A named owner defines how purchase, opt-out and recent-contact signals cross the boundary</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Launch one eligible channel first</td><td>Permission records or the second channel are incomplete</td><td>The launch audience excludes unresolved records and the second channel stays paused</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Do not assume an internal journey setting controls another provider's queue. A Shopify reminder, a retained ESP, a welcome offer and a recovery text can all compete for the same order. Write down which flow takes priority and what evidence makes a shopper ineligible for the others. If that evidence cannot arrive reliably across systems, simplify the design before increasing message volume. Sequenzy or another proposed lifecycle layer belongs in this decision only after its required event and suppression coverage is demonstrated; adding a tool does not by itself solve ownership.</p>
        <h2>Reconcile subscriber state before rebuilding flows</h2>
        <p>Inventory files you already retained from Yotpo, Shopify customer records, suppression lists and the evidence attached to each permission. Keep email and phone eligibility separate. A shopper who opted into one channel should not silently become eligible for both through a contact import. Where source records disagree, quarantine the disputed channel until the owner resolves the record rather than selecting the most permissive value.</p>
        <p>Create a migration mapping with source identifier, destination identifier, channel, status, evidence location and last relevant update. Preserve opt-outs alongside active subscriptions. Ask the receiving provider to review the intended import and sender setup. Do not promise historical cart replay, a fresh Yotpo export or automatic transfer of all old reporting. If records cannot be recovered, the launch plan must work without them.</p>
        <h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: one shopper, several buying states</h2>
        <p>This is a proposed controlled exercise, not a report of tests performed. Use test identities representing email-only, SMS-only, both-channel and opted-out shoppers. Record each event, eligibility decision and expected sender. Start with a product view, then add a variant, then proceed to checkout. Attentive's <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4410320387732-Email-abandonment-journeys">email abandonment guide</a> distinguishes browse, cart and checkout journeys and describes prioritizing the latest buying stage when those email journeys coexist. Verify that behavior in your configuration; it is not proof that external email or SMS queues are also suppressed.</p>
        <p>Next, purchase before a scheduled reminder and check that the pending recovery path stops. Repeat with a shopper who opts out while waiting, one who changes quantity and one whose selected variant becomes unavailable. Include a second active sender in the exercise if you intend to keep it. A pass requires the expected eligible recipient, current content, a working return path and no conflicting follow-up. A duplicate or post-purchase reminder is a routing failure to fix before opening the audience.</p>
        <h2>Cart restoration deserves its own check</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/19809692669972-Use-Cart-URL-personalizations-in-journey-messages">cart URL guide</a> documents subscriber-specific Shopify cart permalinks, later cart additions and a static fallback if a permalink cannot be generated. That differs from freezing a checkout snapshot. Compare the rendered product, the link destination and the eventual basket after changing a variant or adding another item. Also test an empty or unavailable basket so a fallback does not turn a specific reminder into a confusing dead end.</p>
        <p>Check coupon eligibility in the actual basket, including a welcome discount or bundle already applied. Link personalization is not evidence that a discount can stack, that a subscription price is correct or that stock is reserved. Prefer copy that explains a genuine shipping or returns concern to a timer that invents scarcity.</p>
        <h2>Compare contribution and operating cost</h2>
        <p>Request a scoped quote for intended countries, channel usage, email access, onboarding and any services you require. Model the eligible audience after permission and purchase exclusions, rather than multiplying total contacts by a guessed message cadence. Include the work of maintaining cross-provider suppression if email stays elsewhere. No fixed price or return on investment is established here.</p>
        <p>For a hypothetical $441 order with $198.45 of product cost, contribution before other variable costs is $242.55. A 17% discount removes $74.97 of revenue, leaving $167.58 before delivery, payment, messaging and other costs. Applying 17% only to the original contribution would understate the incentive cost. This example explains the calculation; it is neither a recommended offer nor an observed recovery result.</p>
        <p>Agree on attribution windows and distinguish orders following a message from orders caused by it. If audience size permits a meaningful experiment, plan a holdout with the analyst rather than prescribing a universal percentage. Review contribution, opt-outs, complaints and operational failures alongside attributed revenue. A lower attributed total can still be preferable if the program avoids unnecessary discounts and conflicting reminders.</p>
        <h2>Cut over by explicit readiness criteria</h2>
        <p>Before enabling a live journey, assign an owner to subscriber reconciliation, domain and sender setup, event validation, templates and competing-flow removal. Keep a record of the old flows you can still access and any outstanding historical gaps. Replace obsolete Yotpo references in sign-up units and customer-facing copy without implying that permissions have been recreated.</p>
        <p>Proceed when the scoped channel access is confirmed and the controlled exercises pass. If event coverage works but cross-provider exclusions fail, launch the simpler ownership model or keep the affected path paused. If permission evidence is missing, narrow the audience. These are practical go/no-go decisions, not evidence that this site has tested a merchant account or cleared a migration for publication.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Yotpo to Attentive migration FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
